WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:11.960 align:start position:0%
 
Alleluia Christ is risen  Christ is risen Alleluia Christ is risen  oh no no one more time always three  Alleluia Christ is risen  welcome that it's great to have you here  on Easter Sunday  I miss the hats and the white gloves of  my youth in the south  I missed the bonnets and the white  patent leather shoes all those great  things but it is great to see at least  one hat today and it takes me back to a  warm place our guides to Easter today  are ambiguous ones st. Luke writes that  on the first day of the week at early  dawn the women came to the tomb taking  the spices that they had prepared the  women named later in Luke's Easter story  as Mary Joanna and Mary Magdalene and  others that are unnamed seem to be lots  of Mary's going around that these are  uncertain guards to the resurrection  because well they weren't planning on a  resurrection when they came to the tomb  that morning they were coming to a  funeral and their spices gave them away  so I want you to think this Easter  morning what do those spices mean in the  ancient world when everything had fallen  apart still you could bring spices where  I grew up in West Texas when everything  fell apart you would bring a casserole  it was hard to think of an occasion even  dire and shocking events for which a  casserole was not an appropriate  response after all we said people have  to eat in the same way and long ago  Jerusalem dead bodies had to be spiced  no matter how beloved or remarkable the  person who had died no matter how awful  the circumstances of their death it was  only proper to bring spices to scent and  preserve the body it was what grieving  people did for someone they loved but  those spices they were a sure sign that  you were grieving and you did not expect  that grief to end  the women weren't rooting against the  resurrection don't don't take it there  they more than anyone wanted him to rise  but they saw him die on the cross that  Friday and they knew from bitter  lifelong experience that this was the  way the world was the good die young and  the evil prosper those are the rules and  the spices were assigned that they  believed in those rules though they  wanted to believe Jesus and all the  wonderful things he said still the world  grinds on and as we hear from each other  entirely too often you got to be  realistic but when they got to the  garden the tomb was open which it  shouldn't have been and inside there was  no body to be perfumed and two strange  men appeared which i think is always a  little scary and asked the women these  grieving women some very unsympathetic  questions why do you look for the living  amongst the dead he is not here he has  been raised don't you remember what he  said that he would be betrayed and  crucified but would be raised why are  you looking for the living amongst the  dead why are you here in the graveyard  with your spices and I think the women  with their jars of myrrh and aloes  whatever it was I think they look down  at their hands at that moment as though  the spices had suddenly appeared from  nowhere spices  what spices we were making tabbouleh  we were just in the neighborhood on a  beautiful Easter morning in New Haven  Connecticut we come like the women I  think to the tomb we know what Jesus  said we know he said he would suffer and  die and rise again in three days but I  wonder if we have our own spices tucked  away we want to believe what he said not  just about dying and rising but the  whole package of what he said the part  about the poor and the lowly being  lifted up and the high and the mighty  being cast down that's a good part and  the part about the merciful and the poor  and the peacemakers being blessed by God  that's great  we want to believe it we want to believe  a kingdom of justice and peace is coming  like the women we've seen what with our  own eyes how the world treats the  innocent and the dreamers we want to  believe rising from the dead that might  be a step too far I think we all have  our own spices tucked away signs that we  are still grieving and unsure that in  this terrible world in many ways that  there is hope of life and resurrection  we want all the wonderful things Jesus  said to be true but we hedge our bets  our commitments are with death our  spices are the reverse of Pascal's wager  we have them just in case God doesn't  come through Marcus Borg has said the  dominant values of our world are all  things of death affluence personal  achievement can I say that again  appearance power competition consumption  individualism violence all these things  that lead us away from the kingdom Jesus  described a kingdom where weakness is  strength he said where profit can only  be made by giving away everything you  have where people are loved for their  wounds rather than their  parents and their money we succumb to  often to the inevitabilities of this  world of life stealing realities  sometimes we even give in and accept  that this world of competition and power  and isolation is all there is we forget  that God loved this world and all of us  so much that God became one of us that  each human life your life my life was  worth dying for  we want to believe and yet on this  Easter morning we find ourselves at the  open door of the tomb and the men say  why do you look for the living amongst  the dead and we feel something down in  our pockets  yeah we heard Jesus say look at the  lilies of the field he said  and don't worry for tomorrow but we have  our spices to keep us company in this  dying world and we worry and our parents  worry for us and so we bring spices like  an elite education which for some is a  guarantee of security more powerful and  more real than any of God's promises  that we don't have to live like those  poor people or perhaps even see them  those are our spices  perhaps I do believe what Jesus said  about loving my enemies but still I want  to hold back it's so much fun to have  enemies  to get together with my friends and talk  about how stupid and obnoxious they are  whether that's an Old Campus on an ell  campus suite or in my office or in the  US Congress I want to believe that all  human beings are my brothers and sisters  but I hold on to my spices and well  maybe even one more  I believe that the story of Jesus in my  heart I believe that it's a life given  even life-saving story and that when  it's invited I should offer it to other  people who are suffering but that's just  too religious and no one likes religious  people we could keep going through the  whole spice rack as many as you're  willing to imagine all the ways we live  our lives as though Jesus was still in  the tomb and never rose all the ways we  live as though the mighty will not be  cast down the lowly lifted up all the  ways we live as though the world will  always be as it is unjust corrupt  pointless and violent but the Jesus I  know from the scriptures he wouldn't  hold our spices against us he knows  we've got them if Jesus were to meet us  in the garden on Easter morning and saw  us trying to hide our spices behind our  backs he would laugh and he would hold  out his hands until we like guilty  schoolchildren coughed up every jar  Jesus knows we have to live in a world  that thinks God's good news is a fairy  tale in a world that's learned from  terror and wars on terror from greed  fueled economic meltdowns from  idealistic Crusades that turned into  Crusades a world that has learned that  we should be hard-nosed realists and not  give our hearts away to any dreams but  the empty tomb is not a dream even the  disciples who doubted the women came to  believe that at least once something  impossible did happen and that one  impossible thing changed everything they  remember what he said that the human one  the child of God the Lamb of God would  have to suffer and die because death  does rule the world and yet having been  crushed by the worst the world had to  give he would be pulled out of that dark  tomb by the hand  of God and would rise to push death off  the throne and rein in mercy and joy  forever we come to the tomb bearing our  spices faintly hoping that we will not  find a bloody corpse but pretty well  convinced that's what we will find but  he is not there  he is risen an old friend told me about  a man he knew in his church who was an  unlikely Christian he's a world-renowned  biochemist who was very quiet about his  faith but solid and in an unguarded  moment the man told my friend that he  struggled with belief especially in  miracles and resurrection and crazy  things like that but once at an  especially tough time in his life he  resolved to do a simple thing for his  faith a simple daily thing and no one  knew he did it except his wife and his  children he resolved that every morning  when he rolled out of bed and his feet  hit the floor he would remember to sing  to himself he said he didn't always  remember but some days it made all the  difference about the day his day went in  the lab or teaching and her  administration that indeed Jesus Christ  was risen that day my friend said he saw  the man at a memorial service for a  church member who had been killed in the  World Trade Center on September 11th  2001 and with a very sad look on his  face he said the man gently clasped him  by the shoulder and hummed in his ear  and said even today and again years  later when the man's mother died my  friend saw him in the hospital and  through his tears again the man hummed  and said especially today finally the  man's wife told him that after her  husband had died a difficult death in  the hospital she and his children  clasped each other's hands together  resurrection means a fundamental  reorientation of the universe away from  death and towards life in its fullness  it means a fundamental reorientation of  our lives away from meaninglessness and  a cold universe where suffering is  always just suffering two universe that  is in spite of so much of our experience  powerfully tending towards Grace and joy  and healing it is a faith we live in to  some with a radical leap some with a  daily song it is a faith that will never  be popular in this world and that others  will always question but over time we  find that we don't need to hedge our  bets we don't need our spices because  over and over again in our lives and in  the lives of our brothers and sisters we  have seen Christ rise and one by one we  can let go of our little jars our  security blankets are comforting  pessimism and look up and see God's  kingdom of justice and peace coming to  earth with the risen Christ at its head  hallelujah Christ is risen Alleluia  Christ is risen  Alleluia Christ is risen

